{
  "title": "The print() Command — Your First Spell",
  "subtitle": "Speaking to the World",
  "topic": "1.2 • THE PRINT() COMMAND",
  "body": "<div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; width: 100%;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; margin-bottom: 8px; width: 100%;\"><div style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 4px 4px 0px #111111; padding: 4px 14px; margin-bottom: 12px; font-weight: 900; font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px;\">1.2 • Python Foundations</div><h1 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 32px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: -0.5px;\">The <hlt>print()</hlt> Command — Your First Spell</h1><p style=\"font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; color: #222222; margin: 0 auto; max-width: 600px;\">In programming, <hlt>print()</hlt> is how your code communicates and displays messages on the screen.</p></div><div style=\"background: #fef08a; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 22px; box-sizing: border-box;\"><span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">1. The Mail Slot Analogy</span><h2 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 10px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">Sending Messages to the Screen</h2><p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 12px 0; line-height: 1.5;\">Think of <hlt>print()</hlt> as a mail slot connected directly to the output console. Whatever you place inside the parentheses gets delivered and shown on the screen.</p><div style=\"background: #0d1117; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #111111; padding: 12px 16px; font-family: 'Consolas', monospace; color: #f0f6fc; font-size: 14px; border-radius: 4px;\"><span style=\"color: #f472b6;\">print</span>(<span style=\"color: #a5d6ff;\">\"Hellow\"</span>) &nbsp;<span style=\"color: #8b949e;\"># Outputs: Hellow</span></div></div><div style=\"background: #93c5fd; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 22px; box-sizing: border-box;\"><span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">2. Text (Strings) vs Numbers (Integers)</span><h2 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 10px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">Quotes Matter!</h2><p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 12px 0; line-height: 1.5;\">In Python, regular text must always be wrapped in quotation marks (<hlt>\"...\"</hlt> or <hlt>'...'</hlt>). This creates a <strong>string (str)</strong>. But pure numbers, like integer <hlt>5</hlt>, do <strong>NOT</strong> need quotes!</p><div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 12px;\"><div style=\"background: #ffffff; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #111111; padding: 12px 14px;\"><strong style=\"font-family: 'Title', monospace; color: #0284c7;\">Text String (str)</strong><div style=\"font-family: monospace; font-size: 13px; margin-top: 6px; background: #f1f5f9; padding: 6px; border-radius: 3px;\">print(\"Hellow\")</div><p style=\"font-size: 12px; margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #475569;\">Quotes tell Python: 'This is literal text, do not treat it as a variable name.'</p></div><div style=\"background: #ffffff; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #111111; padding: 12px 14px;\"><strong style=\"font-family: 'Title', monospace; color: #16a34a;\">Integer Number (int)</strong><div style=\"font-family: monospace; font-size: 13px; margin-top: 6px; background: #f1f5f9; padding: 6px; border-radius: 3px;\">print(5)</div><p style=\"font-size: 12px; margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #475569;\">No quotes! Python knows 5 is a real integer number ready for math calculations.</p></div></div></div><div style=\"background: #86efac; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 22px; box-sizing: border-box;\"><span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">3. Printing Character Sequences</span><h2 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 10px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">Sequences & Multiple Items</h2><p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 12px 0; line-height: 1.5;\">You can print characters separated by spaces in a single string or pass multiple items to <hlt>print()</hlt> separated by commas.</p><div style=\"background: #0d1117; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #111111; padding: 12px 16px; font-family: 'Consolas', monospace; color: #f0f6fc; font-size: 14px; border-radius: 4px;\"><span style=\"color: #f472b6;\">print</span>(<span style=\"color: #a5d6ff;\">\"P Y T H O N\"</span>)<br/><span style=\"color: #8b949e;\"># Python displays each character in sequence!</span></div></div><div style=\"background-color: #c4b5fd; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 16px 20px; text-align: center;\"><p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">⚡ Ready to Code: On the next page, you'll complete 3 live coding challenges in the interactive editor!</p></div></div>"
}
